
Lent is often described as a season of “giving something up.”
Chocolate. Social media. Snacks. Comforts.

But if you listen closely to the heartbeat of the Church…
Lent is not really about giving up.
Lent is about making space.

When we give something up, we’re not trying to prove our strength.
We’re not trying to impress God with our discipline.
We’re not trying to survive forty days of deprivation.

The real invitation is much deeper.

Lent asks us to look at our lives and gently ask:
What is taking up space in my heart?
What is crowding out my peace?
What is filling the silence where God wants to speak?

Because every “no” in Lent is meant to create room for a greater “yes.”

When you fast from noise, you make space for clarity.
When you fast from distraction, you make space for prayer.
When you fast from comfort, you make space for courage.
When you fast from habits that numb you, you make space for healing.

Lent is not a season of shrinking.
It’s a season of opening.

It’s God whispering:
“Let Me in.
Make room for Me.
Let Me reorder what matters.”

So if you’re entering this Lent feeling unsure of what to give up…
Maybe start here:

Ask yourself,
“Where do I need more space for God?”

And let your practice flow from that question.

Because Lent isn’t about losing something.
It’s about discovering Someone.
It’s about clearing the clutter so grace can breathe again.
It’s about making space — in your mind, in your habits, in your heart —
for the God who has been waiting for you all along.


This Lent, don’t just give something up.
Make space.
And let God fill it.
